# {{ service_name }} architecture

The process lifecycle, settings, resources, health endpoints, and optional PostgreSQL transaction boundary come from the pinned generic FastAPI source. This specialization adds only the Pokerlost root/OpenAPI protocol, SDK dependency, and fail-closed authorization boundary. No business route is registered.

`GET /` returns `PokerlostResponse[None]`; `GET /getOpenApi` publishes the actual OpenAPI document. Future operations use one lower camelCase `POST /<operationId>` with typed `PokerlostRequest` and `PokerlostResponse`. A protected operation requires exact local capability scopes and a real local verifier. The pristine verifier is absent and fails closed. No SDK generator contract is inferred from missing business routes.

The generated project owns `SERVICE.md`, `PROJECT.md`, concrete routes, scopes, adapters, and migrations. Review template updates against those owners. Historical migrations can create historical tables in a new database; changing this code foundation does not clean storage.

## Runtime and execution settings

`ServiceSettings.environment` selects `main`, `dev`, `test`, or `local` (the default)
through `{{ projectName | upper }}_RUNTIME_ENVIRONMENT` or an explicit constructor
argument. The existing telemetry input `{{ projectName | upper }}_ENVIRONMENT`
keeps `local`, `dev`, `ci`, and `remote` as independent execution contours.

Constructor inputs retain priority over process variables, then an explicitly
selected dotenv file or secret directory. Neither identity derives from Git or
configures endpoints, credentials, databases, namespaces, routing, or authorization.
Generated application settings remain project-owned during updates. The pinned
generic FastAPI source owns these settings and their managed regression tests.
